---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A virtual Pop It is a bubble board you tap in the browser instead of squeezing silicone. It is
      free, needs no download, and runs in the tab with no app and no account. Open the page, tap a
      bubble, watch it deflate, and keep going until the grid is clear. Then hit Reset board and
      start again. Turn on Infinite mode when you want a scrollable field that grows as you go.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example: Input: a phone-width tab and a scrollable field of inflated bubbles. Output: a
      quiet sensory loop with optional sound and haptics, no app install, and nothing uploaded about
      which bubbles you popped.
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore this page is a virtual fidget you can use on a call with sound muted, or on a phone
      with haptics on, without granting notification permissions.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Pop It →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-is">
    <h2>What is a virtual Pop It? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A physical Pop It is a silicone tray of bubbles you push through for the click and the feel.
      A virtual pop it online is the same gesture as a grid of buttons: each cell starts raised,
      and a tap leaves it flat until you reset the board.
    </p>
    <p>
      Search results mix this with virtual bubble wrap. Wrap pops once and stays dead. A Pop It
      fidget toy is reversible: you finish one side, turn it over, and the bubbles are ready again.
      This page is that loop as buttons, not a sheet of wrap, and Reset is the digital flip.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, the useful part is not the picture of silicone. It is that a browser tab needs no
      install, no account, and no ad-heavy clone that autoplays audio. Preferences for sound and
      vibration live under Settings → Feel and apply to every fidget on ToyTools.
    </p>
    <p>
      People search for a pop it sensory toy when they want something to do with their hands while
      thinking. A digital pop it, a bubble pop fidget, or a plain popit by another name answers that
      without another app icon on the home screen. A fidget pop it in the browser is still a
      pop-it: the hyphen is packaging, the tap is the product.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How does this Pop It online work? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The board opens as a four by three field sized for a phone-width screen. With Infinite mode
      on, it grows by more rows as you scroll. Tap one bubble and three things happen in order.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bubble marks itself popped so a second event on the same press cannot fire again.
      The Feel engine plays a short pop cue if Sounds are on.
      If Haptics are opted in, the same call asks the phone to vibrate once, and a missing
      vibrator is ignored instead of throwing.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, on a laptop with haptics left off, you still see the bubble flatten and may hear
      the soft cue. On a phone with haptics on, you get the buzz as well. Sound and vibration stay
      independent, therefore muting for an open office does not turn vibration off for later.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reduced motion (from your system or Settings → Feel) turns off bubble transitions. Taps still
      pop bubbles and Reset still works. Once Feel loads, the Full or Reduced choice under
      Settings → Feel overrides the system preference.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sound">
    <h2>How do online Pop It fidgets handle sound? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Badly, on many pages. Always-on audio is fine alone and unusable on a shared desk. Optional
      haptics vs always-on vibration is the same problem with a different API: desktop browsers
      often have no vibrator, and some toys call <code>navigator.vibrate</code> without catching a
      rejection, which is why some Pop It pages break when vibration is unsupported.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here, both channels go through <code>ToyTools.feel</code>. The pop cue is a short tone the
      browser synthesises. There is no audio file to download. Sounds default on; haptics default
      off. Change either under Settings → Feel. A rejected haptic promise never stops the pop
      animation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore you can mute Pop It sounds but keep haptics for a phone session, or the reverse on
      a quiet call. That is how to use a Pop It online without installing an app and without
      fighting the page for control of your speakers.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Leaving sound on in a shared space">
      <p>
        Blaming the page for every click ignores the Settings → Feel toggle. Mute once and every
        fidget on the site stays quiet, including this one.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Expecting desktop vibration">
      <p>
        Most laptops have no vibrator. Haptics stay off by default, and even when opted in a missing
        API is ignored so the bubble still flattens.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="reset">
    <h2>How to reset a finished Pop It board <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      When every bubble is flat, tap Reset board, or turn on Infinite mode and scroll for more rows.
      Reset inflates every loaded bubble, scrolls the field back to the top, and leaves your Feel
      preferences alone. Reloading the tab when Reset would continue the session is the common
      mistake this control exists to remove.
    </p>
    <p>
      Infinite mode hides the trust row, knowledge drawers, footer, and every Pop It control so only
      the scrollable board remains. ToolBar stays for landmarks. Sound and Haptics toggles under the
      board stay in sync with Settings → Feel on the finite page; Infinite is board-only, so set them
      before you enter or use Settings → Feel. Exit or Escape brings the finite board and the rest of
      the page back.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Reload is not Reset">
      <p>
        A finished board looks like a dead end. Refreshing the page works, but it also drops focus,
        reloads assets, and breaks the fidget loop. Reset board is the continuation: every loaded
        bubble ready again at the top of the field, without leaving the tool.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <p>
      Another failure shows up as double pops: press and release both fire on clones that listen to
      both events. This board marks a bubble popped before feedback runs, so the paired click is a
      no-op.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="when-it-helps">
    <h2>When a browser Pop It helps <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Quietly fidgeting through a long call with sound muted is the usual case. How to play Pop It
      online on a phone is the same grid: open the tab, tap, and turn Haptics on under Settings →
      Feel when you want a buzz. Giving a child a sensory bubble board without ads or accounts is a
      third, because the page is just a grid of buttons.
    </p>
    <p>
      Instead of opening another novelty site with trackers, keep this tab beside a
      <a href={withBase('/tool/productivity/pomodoro-timer/')}>Pomodoro timer</a> and reset between
      short breaks. A bubble fidget and a focus timer answer different needs, however they sit well
      in the same browser session. More toys live in the
      <a href={withBase('/category/fidgets/')}>Fidgets</a> category.
    </p>
    <p>
      Many browser pop-it games put a timer and a best-time board on the same grid. That is a race.
      This page is a fidget: no countdown, no leaderboard, no points. Infinite mode is more board
      when you want it, not a survival clock.
    </p>
    <p>
      Browser Pop It vs a phone fidget app is mostly about trust and friction: the app wants
      permissions and updates; the page asks for a tap. Pick the app only if you need offline icons
      on the home screen. Otherwise the online fidget is enough. Once the tab has loaded, it keeps
      working without a server.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="try">
    <h2>Try the board <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Open the tool, tap a few bubbles, mute sound from the board toggles if you need to, try
      Infinite mode when you want more rows, and reset when you are done. That is the whole product.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Pop It →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
